.nh
.TH Nmstate(3)
Nmstate provides a set of bindings for querying and applying the network state
in the format of YAML.
For properties not mentioned desired state, nmstate will try to preserve
current configuration unless changes required by other desired property.

.PP
For example, to create an OpenvSwitch bridge:

.EX
#!/usr/bin/python3

import yaml
import libnmstate

desire_state = """
interfaces:
- name: eth1
  type: ethernet
  state: up
- name: br0
  type: ovs-bridge
  state: up
  bridge:
    port:
    - name: eth1
    - name: br0
- name: br0
  type: ovs-interface
  state: up
"""

libnmstate.apply(yaml.load(desire_state, Loader=yaml.SafeLoader))
.EE

.EX
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let desire_state: nmstate::NetworkState = serde_yaml::from_str(
        r#"---
interfaces:
- name: eth1
  type: ethernet
  state: up
- name: br0
  type: ovs-bridge
  state: up
  bridge:
    port:
    - name: eth1
    - name: br0
- name: br0
  type: ovs-interface
  state: up
"#,
    )?;
    desire_state.apply()?;
    Ok(())
}

.EE

.PP
TODO: provide example code for using C binding.

.SH Interfaces
The \fBinterfaces\fR section is a list, for those interface not mentioned in desire
state, nmstate will not modify it unless required(for example, bridge port list
changed).

.SS Base Interface
There are many properties that are available for all kind of network
interfaces. The example this is a desire state of base interface properties
only:

.EX
interfaces:
- name: eth1
  profile-name: wan0
  type: ethernet
  state: up
  mac-address: 1C:FF:EE:DD:3B:D3
  permanent-mac-address: 1C:FF:EE:DD:BB:D3
  pci-address: 0000:07:00.0
  mtu: 1500
  min-mtu: 256
  max-mtu: 2304
  wait-ip: any
  accept-all-mac-addresses: false
.EE

.PP
Besides above example, we also have these properties:
 * \fBcontroller\fR
 * \fBcopy-mac-from\fR

.SS Name
The \fBname\fR property is the name string used in kernel or user space tool(for
example OpenvSwitch bridge interface name only exist in its user space
database).

.PP
You may also use this format to refer a SR-IOV VF interface:

.EX
sriov:<pf_name>:<vf_id>
.EE

.PP
Please check this document
\[la]../features/iface_vf_id.md\[ra] for detail.

.SS Type
The \fBtype\fR property is the type name of interface. Please read on to get all
supported interface types.

.SS State
All possible states of interface are:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBup\fR: Indicate network backend is managing this interface and activated
configuration of specified interface.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdown\fR: Indicate network backend deactivate configuration of specified
interface.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBignore\fR: Indicate network backend is ignoring this interface. When
applying state with interface marked as \fBignore\fR, the verification will
ignore this interface also.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBabsent\fR: Only used when applying to instruct network backend to delete
configuration of specified interface, this might or might not lead to
the deletion in kernel.

.SS Description
The \fBdescription\fR of the interface. This value can be configured to any string. For example:

.EX
---
interfaces:
- name: eth1
  type: ethernet
  state: up
  description: "Main interface connected to switch S1"
.EE

.SS Profile Name
The profile name used by network backend. For example:

.EX
---
interfaces:
- name: eth1
  profile-name: wan0
  type: veth
  state: up
  identifier: mac-address
  mac-address: 8A:8C:92:1A:F6:98
.EE

.SS Interface Identifier
The \fBidentifier\fR property is only valid for applying or generating
configurations. The valid values are:
 * \fBname\fR: Default value. Specified configuration is applied to interface
   holding the specified interface name.
 * \fBmac-address\fR: Specified configuration is applied to interface holding
   the specified MAC address.
 * \fBpci-address\fR: Specified configuration is applied to interface holding
   the specified PCI address.

.SS MAC Address
The \fBmac-address\fR property holds the MAC address of this interface. Please be
aware the IP over InfiniBand interface have 20 bytes which result in a string
like:

.EX
20:00:55:04:01:FE:80:00:00:00:00:00:00:00:02:C9:02:00:23:13:92
.EE

.PP
The loopback interface will hold MAC address as \fB00:00:00:00:00:00\fR\&.

.PP
When showing, nmstate use upper case string. When applying, it is
case-insensitive.

.PP
For applying or generating configurations:
 * \fBidentifier\fR is undefined or \fBidentifier: name\fR, interface will be changed
   to hold the specified MAC address
 * \fBidentifier: mac-address\fR, interface holding the specified MAC address
   will be used instead of interface name.

.PP
Example on matching interface by MAC address:

.EX
---
interfaces:
  - name: wan0
    type: ethernet
    state: up
    identifier: mac-address
    mac-address: 00:23:45:67:89:1a
    ipv4:
      enabled: true
      dhcp: true
    ipv6:
      enabled: true
      dhcp: true
      autoconf: true
.EE

.SS PCI Address
New since 2.2.47.

.PP
The \fBpci-address\fR property holds the PCI address of this interface.

.PP
When showing, nmstate will use format like \fB0000:07:00.0\fR\&.
When applying, nmstate accept format like \fB07:00.0\fR with domain set to 0.

.PP
When using with \fBidentifier: pci-address\fR, desired state will be applied to
interface holding specified PCI address instead of desired interface name.

.PP
Example on matching interface by PCI address:

.EX
---
interfaces:
  - name: wan0
    type: ethernet
    state: up
    identifier: pci-address
    pci-address: 0000:07:00.0
    ipv4:
      enabled: true
      dhcp: true
    ipv6:
      enabled: true
      dhcp: true
      autoconf: true
.EE

.SS Permanent MAC Address
The \fBpermanent-mac-address\fR property holds the MAC addresses stored in
firmware of network card which will be persistent after system rebooted.

.PP
This is query only property and will be ignored when applying.

.SS MTU
The \fBmtu\fR value is the Maximum Transmission Unit of specified interface.

.PP
It should be in the range between \fBmin_mtu\fR and \fBmax_mtu\fR\&.

.SS Minimum MTU
Available since 2.2.0.

.PP
The minimum MTU supported by specified interface.

.PP
This is query only property and will be ignored when applying.

.SS Maximum MTU
Available since 2.2.0.

.PP
The maximum MTU supported by specified interface.

.PP
This is query only property and will be ignored when applying.

.SS Wait IP
Available since 2.1.3.

.PP
The \fBwait-ip\fR property defines which IP stack should network backend wait
before considering the interface activation finished.

.PP
It supports these values:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBany\fR -- (Default value) The activation is considered done once IPv4 stack
or IPv6 stack is configure.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBipv4\fR -- The activation is considered done once IPv4 stack is configured.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBipv6\fR -- The activation is considered done once IPv6 stack is configured.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBipv4+ipv6\fR -- The activation is considered done once both IPv4 and IPv6
stack are configured.

.SS Interface Controller
Available since 2.2.2.

.PP
The \fBcontroller\fR property defines the controller of the specified interface.
This property is hidden when querying and only used for controller attaching
and detaching. Setting as \fBcontroller: ''\fR means detaching from current
controller.

.PP
This property might conflict with port list of controller, an error will be
trigger when it happens.

.PP
Example yaml for attaching \fBeth1\fR to \fBbr0\fR interface:

.EX
---
interfaces:
- name: eth1
  state: up
  controller: br0
.EE

.SS Accept All MAC addresses
The \fBaccept-all-mac-addresses\fR properties is the boolean switch for enabling
the \fBpromiscuous\fR mode of specified interface which causing the kernel
to accept ethernet packages even its destination MAC address is not for
current interface.

.SS Copy MAC From
The apply only property \fBcopy-mac-from\fR is for clone MAC address of other
interface.

.PP
For example, to create bridge using the MAC address of eth1:

.EX
---
interfaces:
  - name: br0
    type: linux-bridge
    state: up
    copy-mac-from: eth2
    bridge:
      port:
      - name: eth2
      - name: eth1
.EE

.SS Dispatch script
Since 2.2.17, nmstate has introduced support of invoking dispatch script upon
the activation and deactivation of interface.

.PP
For example, this desire state will instruct network backend to invoke
\fBecho post-up-eth1 | systemd-cat\fR after the interface is activated, and
\fBecho post-down-eth1 | systemd-cat\fR after the interface been deactivated.

.EX
---
interfaces:
- name: eth1
  type: ethernet
  state: up
  dispatch:
    post-activation: |
      echo post-up-eth1 | systemd-cat
    post-deactivation: |
      echo post-down-eth1 | systemd-cat
.EE

.PP
Setting the \fBpost-activation\fR or \fBpost-deactivation\fR to empty string will
remove the dispatch scripts. Removing the interface using
\fBstate: absent\fR also remove the dispatch scripts.

.SS Interface Alternative Name
Since 2.2.51, nmstate support configuration interface alternative names.

.PP
Example YAML for adding alternative names:

.EX
---
interfaces:
  - name: enp7s0
    alt-names:
      - name: port1
      - name: veryveryveryverylonglonglongname
.EE

.PP
Example YAML for removing alternative names:

.EX
---
interfaces:
  - name: enp7s0
    alt-names:
      - name: port1
        state: absent
      - name: veryveryveryverylonglonglongname
        state: absent
.EE

.PP
Considering the alternative name might also added by external tools(e.g.
systemd-udev), nmstate is doing incremental changes to interface alternative
names, meaning:
 * Applying YAML only append alt-names to existing.
 * Only remove names marked with \fBstate: absent\fR\&.

.SS IP
This is the example of interface with static IP addresses:

.EX
---
interfaces:
- name: eth1
  state: up
  ipv4:
    enabled: true
    dhcp: false
    address:
    - ip: 192.0.2.252
      prefix-length: 24
      mptcp-flags:
      - signal
      - subflow
    - ip: 192.0.2.251
      prefix-length: 24
      mptcp-flags:
      - signal
      - subflow
  ipv6:
    enabled: true
    autoconf: false
    dhcp: false
    address:
    - ip: 2001:db8:2::1
      prefix-length: 64
      mptcp-flags:
      - signal
      - subflow
    - ip: 2001:db8:1::1
      prefix-length: 64
      mptcp-flags:
      - signal
      - subflow
.EE

.PP
This is the example of interface with dynamic IP addresses:

.EX
---
interfaces:
  - name: eth1
    type: ethernet
    state: up
    ipv4:
      enabled: true
      dhcp: true
      auto-dns: true
      auto-gateway: true
      auto-routes: true
      auto-route-table-id: 0
      auto-route-metric: 101
      dhcp-client-id: ll
    ipv6:
      enabled: true
      dhcp: true
      autoconf: true
      auto-dns: true
      auto-gateway: true
      auto-routes: true
      auto-route-table-id: 0
      auto-route-metric: 102
      dhcp-duid: ll
.EE

.SS IP Enable
The \fBenabled\fR property is the boolean switch to enable and disable the whole IP
stack.

.PP
For IPv6 with \fBenabled: false\fR, the link local address will also be removed
from this interface.

.PP
If explicitly set to \fBenabled: false\fR, all other IP parameters will be ignored
and will not fail the verification.

.SS DHCP
The \fBdhcp\fR property is the boolean switch for DHCP on specified interface.

.PP
When set to \fBdhcp: false\fR for IPv4, these properties will be ignored and will
not fail the verification:
 * \fBauto-dns\fR
 * \fBauto-gateway\fR
 * \fBauto-routes\fR
 * \fBauto-route-table-id\fR
 * \fBauto-route-metric\fR
 * \fBdhcp-client-id\fR

.PP
When dynamic configuration disabled(IPv4 dhcp off, IPv6 dhcp off and autoconf
off),  these properties will be ignored and will not fail the verification:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBauto-dns\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBauto-gateway\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBauto-routes\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBauto-route-table-id\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBauto-route-metric\fR

.SS IPv6 Autoconf
The \fBautoconf\fR property is the boolean switch for IPv6 autoconf via Router
Advertisement (RA).

.PP
Limitation:
.IP \(bu 2
Currently nmstate does not allows \fBautoconf: true\fR and \fBdhcp: false\fR\&.

.SS IP Address
The \fBaddress\fR property holds a list of object for IP address which contains
these parameters:
 * \fBip\fR
 * \fBprefix-length\fR
 * \fBmptcp-flags\fR -- Query only, list of MPTCP flag string.

.PP
The order of IP address will be preserved when applying to kernel.

.SS Auto DNS
The \fBauto-dns\fR property is the boolean switch for whether apply DNS
configurations retried from dynamic configuration method.

.PP
Set to true by default unless current state set to false explicitly.

.SS Auto Routes
The \fBauto-routes\fR property is the boolean switch for whether apply routes
entries(including default gateway) retried from dynamic configuration method.

.PP
Set to true by default unless current state set to false explicitly.

.SS Auto Gateway
The \fBauto-routes\fR property is the boolean switch for whether apply default
gateway retried from dynamic configuration method.

.PP
Set to true by default unless current state set to false explicitly.

.SS Auto Route Table ID
The \fBauto-route-table-id\fR property is the integer for route table ID of
the routes retried from dynamic configuration method.

.PP
If not mentioned in desire state or set to 0, nmstate will use the main table
ID 254.

.SS Auto Route Metric
Available since nmstate 2.2.1.

.PP
The \fBauto-route-metric\fR property is the integer for route metric of
the routes retried from dynamic configuration method.

.PP
If not mentioned in desire state or set to -1, nmstate will let network backend
to determine the metric.

.SS DHCPv4 Client ID
Available since nmstate 2.1.1.

.PP
The \fBdhcp-client-id\fR property accepts string as arbitrary value, these special
value are treat specially:
 * \fBll\fR or \fBLL\fR: Use link layer address as DHCPv4 client ID.
 * \fBiaid+duid\fR or \fBIAID+DUID\fR: RFC 4361 type 255, 32 bits IAID followed by
   DUID.

.SS DHCPv6 Unique Identifier
Available since nmstate 2.1.1.

.PP
The \fBdhcp-duid\fR property accepts string as arbitrary value, these special
value are treat specially:
 * \fBll\fR or \fBLL\fR: Use link layer address
 * \fBllt\fR or \fBLLT\fR: DUID Based on Link-Layer Address Plus Time
 * \fBen\fR or \fBEN\fR: DUID Assigned by Vendor Based on Enterprise Number
 * \fBuuid\fR or \fBUUID\fR: DUID Based on Universally Unique Identifier

.SS Multipath TCP
Available since nmstate 2.2.0.

.PP
The \fBmptcp\fR section of interface holds the Multipath TCP interface level
flags, it will apply flags to all valid IP addresses(both static and dynamic).
Network backend might has its own rule on excusing special scope of IP
address, e.g. IPv6 multicast address.

.PP
For example:

.EX
---
interfaces:
  - name: eth1
    type: ethernet
    state: up
    mptcp:
      address-flags:
      - backup
      - signal
    ipv4:
      enabled: true
      dhcp: false
      address:
        - ip: 192.0.2.2
          prefix-length: 24
.EE

.PP
The only supported property of \fBmptcp\fR section is \fBaddress-flags\fR for now.
The \fBaddress-flags\fR property holds the list of MPTCP flags which could be:
 * \fBsignal\fR
 * \fBsubflow\fR
 * \fBbackup\fR
 * \fBfullmess\fR

.PP
Limitations:
 * Per IP address MPTCP flags is ignored with an warning message when
   different from interface level MPTCP flags.
 * Cannot set \fBsignal\fR along with \fBfullmesh\fR due to kernel restriction.
 * If sysctl has \fBnet.mptcp.enabled\fR set to 0, NetworkManager cannot
   apply MPTCP flags. User will get an failure suggest them to use
   third-party tools.
 * Cannot reapply on MPTCP changes, full activation will invoked when
   MPTCP flags changed.
 * When MPTCP not supported by NetworkManager, the query will hide
   interface level MPTCP flags even it been enabled by third party
   tool.

.SS Ethtool
Available since 1.1.0

.PP
The \fBethtool\fR section of interface holds the ethtool settings of network cards,
including these subsections.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBfeature\fR -- configurable offload protocol and other features
.IP \(bu 2
\fBring\fR -- RX/TX ring parameters
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcoalesce\fR -- Coalescing parameters

.PP
This is the example query output of \fBnetdevsim\fR interface:

.EX
interfaces:
- name: sim0
  type: ethernet
  state: up
  ethtool:
    pause:
      rx: true
      tx: true
      autoneg: false
    feature:
      rx-gro: true
      tx-nocache-copy: false
      hw-tc-offload: false
      rx-udp_tunnel-port-offload: true
      tx-generic-segmentation: true
      rx-udp-gro-forwarding: false
      rx-gro-list: false
    coalesce:
      adaptive-rx: false
      adaptive-tx: false
      pkt-rate-high: 0
      pkt-rate-low: 0
      rx-frames: 0
      rx-frames-high: 0
      rx-frames-low: 0
      rx-usecs: 0
      rx-usecs-high: 0
      rx-usecs-irq: 0
      rx-usecs-low: 0
      sample-interval: 0
      stats-block-usecs: 0
      tx-frames: 0
      tx-frames-high: 0
      tx-frames-irq: 0
      tx-frames-low: 0
      tx-usecs: 0
      tx-usecs-high: 0
      tx-usecs-irq: 0
      tx-usecs-low: 0
    ring:
      rx: 0
      rx-max: 4096
      rx-jumbo: 0
      rx-jumbo-max: 4096
      rx-mini: 0
      rx-mini-max: 4096
      tx: 0
      tx-max: 4096
.EE

