.SS Ethtool coalesce
The \fBcoalesce\fR subsection supports these parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBadaptive-rx\fR: boolean
.IP \(bu 2
\fBadaptive-tx\fR: boolean
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpkt-rate-high\fR: 32 bits unsigned integer
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpkt-rate-low\fR: 32 bits unsigned integer
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrx-frames\fR: 32 bits unsigned integer
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrx-frames-high\fR: 32 bits unsigned integer
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrx-frames-low\fR: 32 bits unsigned integer
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrx-usecs\fR: 32 bits unsigned integer
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrx-usecs-high\fR: 32 bits unsigned integer
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrx-usecs-irq\fR: 32 bits unsigned integer
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrx-usecs-low\fR: 32 bits unsigned integer
.IP \(bu 2
\fBsample-interval\fR: 32 bits unsigned integer
.IP \(bu 2
\fBstats-block-usecs\fR: 32 bits unsigned integer
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtx-frames\fR: 32 bits unsigned integer
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtx-frames-high\fR: 32 bits unsigned integer
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtx-frames-irq\fR: 32 bits unsigned integer
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtx-frames-low\fR: 32 bits unsigned integer
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtx-usecs\fR: 32 bits unsigned integer
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtx-usecs-high\fR: 32 bits unsigned integer
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtx-usecs-irq\fR: 32 bits unsigned integer
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtx-usecs-low\fR: 32 bits unsigned integer

.SS Ethtool ring
The \fBring\fR subsection supports these parameters:
 * \fBrx\fR
 * \fBrx-max\fR
 * \fBrx-jumbo\fR
 * \fBrx-jumbo-max\fR
 * \fBrx-mini\fR
 * \fBrx-mini-max\fR
 * \fBtx\fR
 * \fBtx-max\fR

.PP
All of them are 32 bits unsigned integer.

.PP
Example YAML:

.EX
---
interfaces:
  - name: enp1s0
    state: up
    ethtool:
      ring:
        rx: 256
        rx-jumbo: 0
        rx-mini: 0
        tx: 256
.EE

.SS Ethtool FEC(Forward Error Correction)
New in version 2.2.43.

.PP
Example YAML to disable automatic FEC and enforce llrs mode:

.EX
---
interfaces:
  - name: sim0
    type: ethernet
    ethtool:
      fec:
        auto: false
        mode: llrs
.EE

.IP \(bu 2
\fBauto\fR: true or false. Once enabled, the \fBmode\fR setting will be ignored.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmode\fR: valid values are: \fBoff\fR, \fBrs\fR, \fBbaser\fR, \fBllrs\fR\&.

.PP
Please set FEC mode on the switch first, otherwise nmstate verification stage
will complain about desired FEC mode cannot be activated.

.SS LLDP
.SS LLDP Enabled
Indicate whether LLDP listener is enabled for the interface or not. The
property type is \fBboolean\fR\&.

.SS Neighbors
The \fBneighbors\fR section of LLDP holds the parameter of each neighbor reported
by the LLDP listener. This parameters are read-only.

.SS TLV Type
This is the TLV type reported as described in IEEE 802.1AB. The property type
is \fBinteger\fR\&.

.SS TLV Subtype
This is the TLV subtype reported as described in IEEE 802.1AB. The property
type is \fBinteger\fR\&.

.SS Organization code
This is the organization code or \fBoui\fR as described in IEEE 802.1AB. The property
type is \fBinteger\fR\&.

.SS Ethernet Interface
The \fBethernet\fR section of interface holds the parameters of ethernet specific
configurations. For example:

.EX
---
interfaces:
  - name: eth1
    type: ethernet
    state: up
    ethernet:
      speed: 10000
      duplex: full
      auto-negotiation: false
.EE

.SS Ethernet speed
The \fBspeed\fR property is the integer speed in Mbps.

.PP
This property is ignored when applying if \fBauto-negotiation: true\fR\&.

.SS Ethernet duplex
The \fBduplex\fR property supports:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBfull\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBhalf\fR

.PP
This property is ignored when applying if \fBauto-negotiation: true\fR\&.

.SS Ethernet Auto Negotiation
The \fBauto-negotiation\fR property is the boolean switch for enabling
auto negotiation on speed and duplex.

.PP
Undefined means preserving current setting.

.SS SR-IOV
The \fBsr-iov\fR is subsection of \fBethernet\fR interface. For example:

.EX
---
interfaces:
  - name: eth1
    type: ethernet
    state: up
    ethernet:
      sr-iov:
        total-vfs: 2
        vfs:
        - id: 0
          mac-address: 00:11:22:33:00:FF
          spoof-check: true
          trust: false
          min-tx-rate: 0
          max-tx-rate: 0
          vlan-id: 0
          qos: 0
        - id: 1
          mac-address: 00:11:22:33:00:EF
          spoof-check: true
          trust: false
          min-tx-rate: 0
          max-tx-rate: 0
          vlan-id: 0
          qos: 0
.EE

.SS SR-IOV Total VFS Count
The \fBtotal-vfs\fR property defines the SRIOV VF count of PF interface.

.SS SR-IOV VF Specific Settings
The \fBvfs\fR section holds VF specific settings:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBid\fR -- VF ID
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmac-address\fR -- VF MAC address
.IP \(bu 2
\fBspoof-check\fR -- Whether spoof check is enabled
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtrust\fR -- Is Privileged (trusted) VF or not
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmin-tx-rate\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmax-tx-rate\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBvlan-id\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBqos\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBvlan-proto\fR -- choice of \fB802.1q\fR(default) or \fB802.1ad\fR

.PP
Once the \fBvfs\fR section is defined in desire state, user is required to provide
\fBall\fP VFS configuration, nmstate will not merge it with current status.

.SS Bond Interface
The \fBlink-aggregation\fR section of interface holds the parameters of bond
specific configurations. For example:

.EX
---
interfaces:
- name: bond99
  type: bond
  state: up
  link-aggregation:
    mode: balance-rr
    options:
      all_slaves_active: dropped
      arp_all_targets: any
      arp_interval: 0
      arp_validate: none
      downdelay: 0
      lp_interval: 1
      miimon: 100
      min_links: 0
      packets_per_slave: 1
      primary_reselect: always
      resend_igmp: 1
      updelay: 0
      use_carrier: true
    port:
    - eth1
    - eth2
.EE

.SS Bond Mode
The \fBmode\fR property defines the bond mode:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBbalance-rr\fR or 0
.IP \(bu 2
\fBactive-backup\fR or 1
.IP \(bu 2
\fBbalance-xor\fR or 2
.IP \(bu 2
\fBbroadcast\fR or 3
.IP \(bu 2
\fB802.3ad\fR or 4
.IP \(bu 2
\fBbalance-tlb\fR or 5
.IP \(bu 2
\fBbalance-alb\fR or 6

.PP
Please check kernel document for detail meaning of each mode.

.SS Bond Options
The \fBoptions\fR property of bond supports:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBad_actor_sys_prio\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBad_actor_system\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBad_select\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBad_user_port_key\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBall_slaves_active\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBarp_all_targets\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBarp_interval\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBarp_ip_target\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBarp_validate\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdowndelay\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBfail_over_mac\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlacp_rate\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlp_interval\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmiimon\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmin_links\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBnum_grat_arp\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBnum_unsol_na\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpackets_per_slave\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBprimary\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBprimary_reselect\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBresend_igmp\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtlb_dynamic_lb\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBupdelay\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBuse_carrier\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBxmit_hash_policy\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBbalance_slb\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBlacp_active\fR (new in 2.2.47)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBns_ip6_target\fR (new in 2.2.47)

.PP
Please check kernel document for their allowed value and combinations.

.PP
The \fBbalance_slb\fR is not kernel option of bond, but user space configuration,
only supported by nmstate 2.2.1+, NetworkManager 1.41+ on patched linux kernel.

.SS Bond Ports
The \fBport\fR property of bond defines a list of bond ports.

.PP
When defined in desire state, user is required to provide \fBall\fP bond ports.

.PP
You may use \fBports\fR also for applying since 2.1.4.

.SS VLAN Interface
The \fBvlan\fR section of interface holds the parameters of VLAN specific
configurations. For example:

.EX
---
interfaces:
  - name: eth1.101
    type: vlan
    state: up
    vlan:
      base-iface: eth1
      id: 101
      protocol: 802.1q
.EE

.PP
The \fBvlan\fR sections contains two parameters:
 * \fBbase-iface\fR: The parent interface name.
 * \fBid\fR: VLAN ID integer.
 * \fBprotocol\fR: \fB802.1q\fR (default) or \fB802.1ad\fR

.PP
With nmstate 2.2.1+, when using Nmstate with NetworkManager backend, nmstate
can creating VLAN over unmanaged interface, hence if you never mention VLAN
parent in \fBinterfaces\fR section of desired state, nmstate will not convert
VxLAN parent interface from unmanaged to managed.

.PP
For \fBgenerate configuration(gc)\fR mode, unmentioned VLAN parent will not
get automatically created interface profile.

.SS VxLAN Interface
The \fBvxlan\fR section of interface holds the parameters of VxLAN specific
configurations. For example:

.EX
interfaces:
- name: eth1.102
  type: vxlan
  state: up
  ipv6:
    enabled: true
  vxlan:
    base-iface: eth1
    id: 102
    remote: 192.0.2.251
    local: 192.0.2.252
    learning: false
    destination-port: 1235
.EE

.PP
The \fBvxlan\fR sections contains two parameters:
 * \fBbase-iface\fR: The parent interface name.
 * \fBid\fR: VLAN ID integer.
 * \fBremote\fR: The destination IP address to use in outgoing packages when the
   destination link layer address is not known in the VxLAN device forwarding
   database. Could be unicast IP or multicast IP.
 * \fBdestination-port\fR: The UDP destination port.
 * \fBlocal\fR: The source IP address for outgoing packages.
 * \fBlearning\fR: Whether store unknown link-layer address to forward database.

.PP
Currently, we only support VxLAN based on IEEE 802.1Q protocol.

.PP
With nmstate 2.2.1+, when using Nmstate with NetworkManager backend, nmstate
can creating VxLAN over unmanaged interface, if you never mention VxLAN parent
in \fBinterfaces\fR section of desired state, nmstate will not convert VxLAN
parent interface from unmanaged to managed.

.PP
For \fBgenerate configuration(gc)\fR mode, unmentioned VxLAN parent will not
get automatically created interface profile.

.SS Linux Bridge Interface
The \fBbridge\fR section of interface could hold parameters of Linux Bridge
specific configurations. For example:

.EX
interfaces:
- name: br0
  type: linux-bridge
  state: up
  bridge:
    options:
      gc-timer: 29657
      group-addr: 01:80:C2:00:00:00
      group-forward-mask: 0
      group-fwd-mask: 0
      hash-max: 4096
      hello-timer: 0
      mac-ageing-time: 300
      multicast-last-member-count: 2
      multicast-last-member-interval: 100
      multicast-membership-interval: 26000
      multicast-querier: false
      multicast-querier-interval: 25500
      multicast-query-interval: 12500
      multicast-query-response-interval: 1000
      multicast-query-use-ifaddr: false
      multicast-router: auto
      multicast-snooping: true
      multicast-startup-query-count: 2
      multicast-startup-query-interval: 3125
      stp:
        enabled: false
        forward-delay: 15
        hello-time: 2
        max-age: 20
        priority: 32768
      vlan-protocol: 802.1q
    port:
    - name: eth1
      stp-hairpin-mode: false
      stp-path-cost: 100
      stp-priority: 32
.EE

.SS Linux Bridge Options
The \fBoptions\fR subsection of Linux bridge interface \fBbridge\fR section holds these
options:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBgc_timer\fR: Integer. Ignored when applying.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBgroup-addr\fR: String.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBgroup-forward-mask\fR: Integer.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBgroup-fwd-mask\fR: alias of \fBgroup-forward-mask\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBhash-max\fR: Integer.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBhello-timer\fR: Integer. Ignored when applying.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmac-ageing-time\fR: Integer.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmulticast-last-member-count\fR: Integer.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmulticast-last-member-interval\fR: Integer.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmulticast-membership-interval\fR: Integer.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmulticast-querier\fR: Boolean.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmulticast-querier-interval\fR: Integer.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmulticast-query-interval\fR: Integer.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmulticast-query-response-interval\fR: Integer.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmulticast-query-use-ifaddr\fR: Boolean.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmulticast-router\fR: \fBauto\fR, \fBdisabled\fR or \fBenabled\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmulticast-snooping\fR: Boolean.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmulticast-startup-query-count\fR: Integer.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmulticast-startup-query-interval\fR: Integer.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBvlan-default-pvid\fR: Integer.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBstp\fR:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fBenabled\fR: Boolean. Other STP options will be ignored if set \fBfalse\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBforward-delay\fR: Integer
.IP \(bu 2
\fBhello-time\fR: Integer
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmax-age\fR: Integer
.IP \(bu 2
\fBpriority\fR: Integer
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\fBvlan-protocol\fR: \fB802.1q\fR (default) or \fB802.1ad\fR

.SS Linux Bridge Ports
The \fBport\fR subsection of \fBbridge\fR section defines a list of linux bridge ports.

.PP
Each linux bridge port contains these properties:
 * \fBname\fR: Interface name.
 * \fBstp-hairpin-mode\fR: Boolean.
 * \fBstp-path-cost\fR: Integer.
 * \fBstp-priority\fR: Integer.
 * \fBvlan\fR: Please check \fBLinux Bridge Port VLAN\fR section.

.SS Linux Bridge Port VLAN
The \fBvlan\fR subsection of \fBport\fR section of linux bridge holds the VLAN
filtering of linux bridge. For example:

.EX
---
interfaces:
  - name: br0
    type: linux-bridge
    state: up
    bridge:
      port:
        - name: eth1
          vlan:
            mode: trunk
            trunk-tags:
            - id: 101
            - id-range:
                min: 500
                max: 599
            tag: 100
            enable-native: true
        - name: eth2
          vlan:
            mode: trunk
            trunk-tags:
            - id: 500
.EE

.PP
The \fBvlan\fR subsection holds these parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmode\fR: \fBaccess\fR or \fBtrunk\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtag\fR: Integer. VLAN Tag for native VLAN.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBenable-native\fR: Boolean. Enable native VLAN or not.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtrunk-tags\fR: List of VLAN IDs or ID ranges:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fBid\fR: Integer.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBid-range\fR:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmin\fR: Minimum VLAN ID(inclusive).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmax\fR: Maximum VLAN ID(inclusive).
.RE
.RE

.PP
When \fBvlan\fR section not defined in desire state when applying, current VLAN
filtering settings will be preserved for specified interface, once defined,
nmstate will override all VLAN filter settings of specified interface with
desired without merging from current.

.SS OpenvSwitch Bridge Interface
The \fBbridge\fR section of could hold parameters of
OpenvSwitch(Short to OVS below) Bridge specific configurations. For example:

.EX
interfaces:
- name: br0
  type: ovs-bridge
  state: up
  wait-ip: any
  bridge:
    options:
      stp: false
      rstp: false
      mcast-snooping-enable: false
    port:
    - name: ovs0
    - name: eth1
.EE

.SS OpenvSwitch Bridge Options
The \fBoptions\fR subsection of OVS bridge interface \fBbridge\fR section holds these
options:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBstp\fR: Boolean.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrstp\fR: Boolean.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmcast-snooping-enable\fR: String.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBfail-mode\fR: String.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdatapath\fR: String. Only for DPDK.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBallow-extra-patch-ports\fR: Boolean. When set to true, the verification
stage of nmstate will ignore extra OVS patch port not mentioned in \fBport\fR
section. This allows external tool to attach OVS patch port upon nmstate
creating the OVS bridge. This option is not persisted because it only
control nmstate verification stage during apply action. The default value
is false means the verification stage will make sure OVS bridge port list
match with desired state.

.SS OpenvSwitch Bridge Ports
The \fBport\fR subsection of \fBbridge\fR section defines a list of OVS bridge
interfaces.

.PP
There are three type of OVS interfaces:
 * \fBinternal\fR: Represent the bridge itself in kernel.
 * \fBsystem\fR: Interface exists in kernel and attached to this bridge.
 * \fBbond\fR: OVS bond interface.

.PP
This is the example YAML contains these three OVS interface types.

.EX
- name: br0
  type: ovs-bridge
  state: up
  bridge:
    port:
    - name: veth1
    - name: ovs0
    - name: bond1
      link-aggregation:
        mode: balance-slb
        port:
          - name: eth2
          - name: eth1
      vlan:
        mode: access
        tag: 100
.EE

.PP
Each OVS interface config could have:
 * \fBname\fR: String
 * \fBlink-aggregation\fR: Bond config.
 * \fBvlan\fR: Vlan config. Please check \fBLinux Bridge Port VLAN\fR section.

.SS OpenvSwitch DPDK
Below yaml could be used to enable DPDK using PCI device \fB0000:af:00.1\fR:

.EX
---
interfaces:
- name: ovs0
  type: ovs-interface
  state: up
  dpdk:
    devargs: "0000:af:00.1"
    rx-queue: 100
- name: br0
  type: ovs-bridge
  state: up
  bridge:
    options:
      datapath: "netdev"
    port:
    - name: ovs0
ovs-db:
  other_config:
    dpdk-init: "true"
.EE

.PP
Please refer to OpenvSwitch document for technical details.

.SS OpenvSwitch Internal Interface
The OVS internal interface is the virtual interface representing the OVS bridge
in kernel. For example:

.EX
- name: ovs0
  type: ovs-interface
  state: up
  mac-address: 92:7C:8B:BF:AA:D8
  mtu: 1500
  min-mtu: 68
  max-mtu: 65535
  wait-ip: any
  ipv4:
    enabled: false
  ipv6:
    enabled: false
.EE

.PP
There is no special config for OVS internal interface.

.SS OpenvSwitch Bridge Patch Interface
The \fBpatch\fR section of \fBovs-interface\fR could be used for OVS bridge patching.

.PP
This is the example on using OVS patch interface to connect two OVS bridges:

.EX
---
interfaces:
- name: patch0
  type: ovs-interface
  state: up
  patch:
    peer: patch1
- name: ovs-br0
  type: ovs-bridge
  state: up
  bridge:
    port:
    - name: patch0
- name: patch1
  type: ovs-interface
  state: up
  patch:
    peer: patch0
- name: ovs-br1
  type: ovs-bridge
  state: up
  bridge:
    port:
    - name: patch1
.EE

.SS Mac VTAP Interface
The \fBmac-vtap\fR section of \fBmac-vtap\fR interface defines configurations of
MAC VTAP interface.

.PP
For example:

.EX
interfaces:
- name: mac0
  type: mac-vtap
  state: up
  mac-vtap:
    base-iface: eth1
    mode: passthru
    promiscuous: true
.EE

.PP
The \fBmac-vtap\fR section contains these parameters:
 * \fBbase-iface\fR: String. Parent interface name.
 * \fBmode\fR: String. Should be \fBvepa\fR, \fBbridge\fR, \fBprivate\fR, \fBpassthru\fR or
   \fBsource\fR\&.
 * \fBpromiscuous\fR: Boolean.

.SS Mac VLAN Interface
The \fBmac-vlan\fR section of \fBmac-vlan\fR interface defines configurations of
MAC VLAN interface.

.PP
For example:

.EX
interfaces:
- name: mac0
  type: mac-vlan
  state: up
  mac-vlan:
    base-iface: eth1
    mode: passthru
    promiscuous: true
.EE

.PP
The \fBmac-vlan\fR section contains these parameters:
 * \fBbase-iface\fR: String. Parent interface name.
 * \fBmode\fR: String. Should be \fBvepa\fR, \fBbridge\fR, \fBprivate\fR, \fBpassthru\fR or
   \fBsource\fR\&.
 * \fBpromiscuous\fR: Boolean.

.SS IP over InfiniBand Interface
The \fBinfiniband\fR section of \fBinfiniband\fR interface defines configurations of
IP over InfiniBand Interface.

.PP
For example:

.EX
---
interfaces:
  - name: ib2.8001
    type: infiniband
    state: up
    mtu: 1280
    infiniband:
      pkey: "0x8001"
      mode: "connected"
      base-iface: "ib2"
.EE

.PP
The \fBinfiniband\fR sections contains these parameters:
 * \fBpkey\fR: String or integer. P-key of sub-interface. None and \fB0xffff\fR both
   indicate the base interface.
 * \fBbase-iface\fR: Base interface name. Empty for base interface itself.
 * \fBmode\fR: \fBconnected\fR or \fBdatagram\fR\&.

.SS Virtual Routing and Forwarding (VRF) Interface
The \fBvrf\fR section of \fBvrf\fR interface contains configurations of Linux kernel
Virtual Routing and Forwarding(VRF) interface. For example:

.EX
- name: vrf0
  type: vrf
  state: up
  vrf:
    port:
    - eth1
    - eth2
    route-table-id: 100
.EE

.PP
The \fBvrf\fR section contains:
 * \fBport\fR: List of String. VRF Ports.
 * \fBroute-table-id\fR: Which route table ID should this VRF attach to.

.SS Linux Virtual Ethernet(veth) Interface
Besides holding configurations of Ethernet interface, the \fBveth\fR interface
could also hold \fBveth\fR section. For example:

.EX
---
interfaces:
- name: veth1
  type: veth
  state: up
  veth:
    peer: veth1peer
.EE

.SS IPsec Encryption
New feature in 2.2.21

.PP
Nmstate is using Libreswan
\[la]https://libreswan.org/\[ra] daemon and
\fBNetworkManager-libreswan\fR for IPsec encryption communication.

.PP
This is an example of X509 based authentication IPsec connection:

.EX
---
interfaces:
- name: hosta_conn
  type: ipsec
  ipv4:
    enabled: true
    dhcp: true
  libreswan:
    right: 192.0.2.252
    rightid: '@hostb.example.org'
    left: 192.0.2.251
    leftid: '%fromcert'
    leftcert: hosta.example.org
    ikev2: insist
.EE

.PP
The \fBlibreswan\fR section, nmstate provides these properties:
 * \fBnm-auto-defaults\fR: Boolean. New in 2.2.56. Please always explicitly set it
   to \fBfalse\fR for aligning libreswan default values instead of NetworkManager
   default values. Default is true for backwards compatibility.
 * \fBipsec-interface\fR: String 'yes' or 'no' or unsigned integer.
 * \fBauthby\fR: Authentication method. Normally you don't need to set it.
 * \fBdpddelay\fR: Integer.
 * \fBdpdtimeout\fR: Integer.
 * \fBdpdaction\fR: String.
 * \fBikelifetime\fR: String.
 * \fBsalifetime\fR: String.
 * \fBikev2\fR: String.
 * \fBike\fR: String.
 * \fBesp\fR: String.
 * \fBright\fR: String.
 * \fBrightid\fR: String.
 * \fBrightrsasigkey\fR: String.
 * \fBleft\fR: String.
 * \fBleftid\fR: String.
 * \fBleftrsasigkey\fR: String.
 * \fBleftcert\fR: String.
 * \fBikev2\fR: String.
 * \fBpsk\fR: String. The Pre-Shared-Key. Please consider to use x509/PKI
   authentication in production system. In query, this property will be
   shown as \fB<_password_hid_by_nmstate>\fR for security concern.
 * \fBrightsubnet\fR: String. Please explicitly set it to /32 or /128 IP when using
   in host-to-host mode.
 * \fBleftmodecfgclient\fR: true|false. Please explicitly set it to \fBfalse\fR when
   using in host-to-host mode.
 * \fBtype\fR: \fBtransport\fR or \fBtunnel\fR\&. The \fBtunnel\fR is the default value if not
   defined.
 * \fBrightca\fR: String. New in 2.2.56. Certificate Authority (CA) of remote peer.
 * \fBleftprotoport\fR: String. New in 2.2.57. Allowed protocols and ports over
   connection.
 * \fBrightprotoport\fR: String. New in 2.2.57. Allowed protocols and ports over
   connection.

.PP
Except the \fBpsk\fR and \fBnm-auto-defaults\fR properties, all other properties are
libreswan specific options, please refer to the manpage of \fBipsec.conf\fR for
detail meaning of them.

.PP
By default, nmstate will not create any virtual NIC representing the encrypted
communication, they can be check via \fBip xfrm policy\fR command. The IP provided
by IPsec remote will be assigned the interface hosting the underneath network
flow.

.PP
If you prefer a logical interface holding encrypted communication, please set
\fBipsec-interface\fR to \fB\&'yes'\fR or a unsigned integer number, then a xfrm logical
interface named \fBipsec<number>\fR will be created holding the IP retrieved from
IPsec remote.

.PP
You may also check IPsec example page
\[la]../features/ipsec.md\[ra] for use cases.

.SS Loopback Interface
New feature in 2.2.2

.PP
Example loopback interface YAML:

.EX
interfaces:
- name: lo
  type: loopback
  state: up
  mac-address: 00:00:00:00:00:00
  mtu: 65536
  ipv4:
    enabled: true
    dhcp: false
    address:
    - ip: 127.0.0.1
      prefix-length: 8
  ipv6:
    enabled: true
    dhcp: false
    address:
    - ip: ::1
      prefix-length: 128
.EE

.PP
Use \fBstate: absent\fR will revert loopback interface back to kernel defaults.

.PP
Even desired state does not have \fB127.0.0.1/8\fR or \fB::1/128\fR, nmstate will
still include those two IPs to loopback interface.

.SS IPVLAN Interface
New feature in 2.2.38

.PP
Example YAML:

.EX
interfaces:
- name: ipvlan0
  type: ipvlan
  state: up
  ipvlan:
    mode: l3
    base-iface: eth1
    private: false
    vepa: false
.EE

.PP
The \fBipvlan\fR section contains these options:
* \fBmode\fR: String. Should be \fBl2\fR, \fBl3\fR or \fBl3s\fR\&.
    * \fBl2\fR: ipvlan receive and respond to ARP requests, it provides good
      performance, but less control on the network traffic.
    * \fBl3\fR: ipvlan process only L3 traffic and above, does not respond to ARP
      request and users must configure the neighbour entries for the IPVLAN IP
      addresses on the relevant peers manually.
    * \fBl3s\fR: ipvlan process the same way as in L3 mode, except that both egress
      and ingress traffics of a relevant container are landed on netfilter chain
      in the default namespace.
* \fBbase-iface\fR: String. Parent interface name.
* \fBprivate\fR: Boolean. It controls the isolation between the ipvlan interface and
  other devices on the network.
* \fBvepa\fR: Boolean. When enabled, traffic will be forwarded through a central
  switch, helping improve network management and reduce broadcast traffic.

.SS HSR/PRP Interface
New feature since 2.2.22

.PP
HSR (High Availability Seamless Redundancy) and PRP (Parallel Redundancy
Protocol) interface are treat as \fBtype: hsr\fR\&.

.PP
Example YAML for you to create a HSR interface:

.EX
.EE

.SH Routes
The \fBroutes\fR top section of network state contains two type routes:
 * \fBrunning\fR: Running effected routes containing route from universe or link scope,
    and only from these protocols:
    * boot (often used by \fBiproute\fR command)
    * static
    * ra
    * dhcp
    * mrouted
    * keepalived
    * babel
.IP \(bu 2
\fBconfig\fR: Static routes containing route from universe or link scope,
and only from these protocols:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
boot (often used by \fBiproute\fR command)
.IP \(bu 2
static
.RE

.PP
For example:

.EX
routes:
  running:
  - destination: 0.0.0.0/0
    next-hop-interface: eth1
    next-hop-address: 192.0.2.1
    table-id: 254
  config:
  - destination: 0.0.0.0/0
    next-hop-interface: eth1
    next-hop-address: 192.0.2.1
    table-id: 254
.EE

.PP
When applying, the \fBrunning\fR routes are ignored, the \fBconfig\fR routes in desire
state is \fBappended\fP to existing routes. To change or override existing
routes, you need to delete old route entry and add new one(could in single
desire state). For example, to change default gateway of \fBeth1\fR:

.EX
routes:
  config:
  - destination: 0.0.0.0/0
    next-hop-interface: eth1
    state: absent
  - destination: 0.0.0.0/0
    next-hop-address: 192.0.2.1
    next-hop-interface: eth1
.EE

.PP
The \fBstate: absent\fR is used to delete all matching route entry. Below route
will delete all routes with \fBnext-hop-interface: eth1\fR:

.EX
routes:
  config:
  - destination: 0.0.0.0/0
    next-hop-interface: eth1
    state: absent
.EE

.PP
Each route entry could have these parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBstate\fR: Only set to \fBabsent\fR for deleting matching routes when applying.
During querying, nmstate will set routes next hop to ignored interface as
\fBstate: ignore\fR\&. Applying routes with \fBstate: ignore\fR will be ignored.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBdestination\fR: String. Mandatory for every non-absent routes.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBnext-hop-interface\fR: String. Mandatory for every non-absent routes
.IP \(bu 2
\fBnext-hop-address\fR: String. When setting this as empty string for absent
route, it will only delete routes \fBwithout\fP \fBnext-hop-address\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBmetric\fR: Integer. Set to -1 for let backend decide.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBtable-id\fR: Integer. Set to 0 for main table id 254.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBcwnd\fR: Integer. TCP congestion window. According to linux kernel, The
\fBcwnd\fR property of route requires the \fBlock cwnd\fR flag been set at the same
time, otherwise the \fBcwnd\fR will take effect. Hence, on querying, nmstate
treat routes without \fBlock cwnd\fR flag as empty \fBcwnd\fR\&. When applying,
nmstate backend always set \fBlock cwnd\fR\&. If you want to modify existing
\fBcwnd\fR, like modifying other route properties, you need to mark old
route as \fBstate: absnet\fR and append new route.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBvrf-name\fR: String. Store the route to the route table which specified VRF
is binding to.

.SH Route Rules
This is the example of IP route rule:

.EX
route-rules:
  config:
  - ip-from: 192.168.3.2/32
    priority: 1000
    route-table: 200
  - ip-from: 2001:db8:b::/64
    priority: 1000
    route-table: 200
routes:
  config:
  - destination: 192.168.2.0/24
    next-hop-interface: eth1
    next-hop-address: 192.168.1.3
    metric: 108
    table-id: 200
  - destination: 2001:db8:a::/64
    next-hop-interface: eth1
    next-hop-address: 2001:db8:1::2
    metric: 108
    table-id: 200
.EE

.PP
Like \fBroutes\fR, the \fBroute-rules\fR is also using \fBstate: absent\fR for partial
editing. For example:

.EX
---
route-rules:
  config:
    - ip-from: 192.168.3.2/32
      route-table: 200
      state: absent
.EE

.PP
Each route rule entry could contains these parameters:
 * \fBfamily\fR: \fBipv4\fR or \fBipv6\fR\&. Only used when you would like to do
   \fBfrom any to any\fR matching.
 * \fBstate\fR: Only set to \fBabsent\fR for deleting matching route rules.
 * \fBip-from\fR: String. When setting to empty string in absent route rule, it
   will only delete route rule \fBwithout\fP \fBip-from\fR\&.
 * \fBip-to\fR: String. When setting to empty string in absent route rule, it will
   only delete route rule \fBwithout\fP \fBip-to\fR\&.
 * \fBpriority\fR: Integer. Strongly suggested user to manually set priority
   to make sure route rule acting in desired order. If not defined, nmstate
   will add desired routes \fBafter\fP existing route rules.
 * \fBroute-table\fR: Integer. Redirect matching packages to route table ID. Unset
   or 0 will be changed to main table ID 254.
 * \fBfwmark\fR: Integer or hex string. Select the \fBfwmark\fR value to match.
 * \fBfwmask\fR: Integer or hex string. Select the \fBfwmask\fR value to match.
 * \fBiif\fR: String. New in version 2.2.2. Incoming interface name.
 * \fBaction\fR: New in version 2.2.2. \fBblackhole\fR or \fBunreachable\fR or \fBprohibit\fR
   or undefined.

.SH DNS Resolver
The \fBdns-resolver\fR sections contains two sub-sections:
 * \fBrunning\fR: The running effective state. The DNS server might be from DHCP(IPv6
   autoconf) or manual setup.
 * \fBconfig\fR:  The static saved DNS resolver config.  When applying, if this not
   mentioned, current static DNS config will be preserved as it was. If
   defined, will override current static DNS config.

.PP
For example:

.EX
dns-resolver:
  running:
    server:
    - 2001:4860:4860::8888
    - 2001:4860:4860::8844
    - 8.8.4.4
    search:
    - example.com
    - example.org
  config:
    server:
    - 2001:4860:4860::8888
    - 2001:4860:4860::8844
    - 8.8.4.4
    search:
    - example.com
    - example.org
.EE

.PP
NetworkManager backend has two set of DNS configurations:
 * Global DNS set by D-BUS interface or NetworkManager.conf.
 * Interface DNS stored in NetworkManager connection as \fBipv4.dns\fR or
   \fBipv6.dns.\fR

.PP
Nmstate will try to use global DNS via D-BUS interface call, and only use
interface level DNS for any of these use case:
 1. Has IPv6 link-local address as name server: e.g. \fBfe80::deef:1%eth1\fR
 2. User want static DNS server appended before dynamic one. In this case,
    user should define \fBauto-dns: true\fR explicitly along with static DNS.
 3. User want to force DNS server stored in interface for static IP
    interface. This case, user need to state static DNS config along with
    static IP config.

.PP
To purge the static DNS configure, please use

.EX
dns-resolver:
  config: {}
.EE

.PP
Please check DNS feature page for YAML examples
\[la]../features/dns.md\[ra]\&.

.SH Hostname
Only available on nmstate version 2.1.1+.
The \fBhostname\fR section contains these parameters:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBrunning\fR: The running active hostname.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBconfig\fR: The saved static hostname.

.PP
For example:

.EX
hostname:
  running: "c9sr"
  config: "c9ss"
.EE

.PP
To remove static hostname, please set \fBrunning\fR or \fBconfig\fR as empty string.

.SH OpenvSwitch Database
Nmstate supports two types of OpenvSwitch Database modification:
 * Interface level
 * Global level.

.PP
For Interface level, only \fBexternal_ids\fR is supported. For example:

.EX
---
interfaces:
- name: br0
  type: ovs-bridge
  state: up
  bridge:
    port:
    - name: ovs0
    - name: eth1
  ovs-db:
    external_ids:
      gris: 10
- name: ovs0
  type: ovs-interface
  state: up
  ovs-db:
    external_ids:
      gris: abc
- name: eth1
  ovs-db:
    external_ids:
      gris: xyz
.EE

.PP
For global config, both \fBexternal_ids\fR and \fBother_config\fR are supported.
For example:

.EX
ovs-db:
  other_config:
    stats-update-interval: "1000"
  external_ids:
    ovn-localnet-bridge-mappings: "ovn-external:breth0"
.EE
